     1	# Legacy SOCKS multi-tunnel contract
     2	
     3	**Contract version:** 1.1
     4	**Status:** shipped product behavior in Relux Proxy v0.2.0; production GitLab
     5	publication remains gated on the external release prerequisites
     6	**Applies to:** the system-OpenSSH SOCKS product only; it does not define the
     7	future system VPN.
     8	
     9	This is the source of truth for the v0.2.0 extension of the macOS menu-bar SOCKS
    10	app from one tunnel to independently controlled, concurrent tunnels. The
    11	behavior described here is implemented and accepted in the v0.2.0 source
    12	candidate; it is no longer planned-only work. Production distribution is a
    13	separate evidence gate: as of 2026-07-28, no GitLab release is claimed because
    14	the Relux-owned GitLab project and authorization have not been supplied. The
    15	future VPN remains governed by the other documents in this directory.
    16	
    17	The key words **MUST**, **SHOULD**, and **MAY** are normative.
    18	
    19	## 1. Product boundary
    20	
    21	A user MUST be able to save two or more SSH SOCKS profiles, choose each local
    22	SOCKS port explicitly, and run profiles concurrently when their ports differ.
    23	Each profile is independently started, stopped, diagnosed, edited, and removed.
    24	
    25	Every tunnel MUST use the macOS system executable /usr/bin/ssh. Relux Proxy
    26	does not parse, rewrite, import, or reimplement OpenSSH configuration semantics.
    27	The app remains a manual, local-only SOCKS endpoint: users configure a
    28	SOCKS-aware client themselves.
    29	
    30	The current one-tunnel implementation and its sshHost, sshAccount, and
    31	localPort preferences are migration inputs, not a second live configuration
    32	model after migration.
    33	
    34	## 2. Persisted profile model
    35	
    36	### 2.1 Versioned store
    37	
    38	The app MUST store the profile collection as one JSON Data value in the standard
    39	app UserDefaults suite under the key legacySocksProfiles.v1. The payload has
    40	this exact v1 shape:
    41	
    42	    {
    43	      "schemaVersion": 1,
    44	      "profiles": [
    45	        {
    46	          "id": "4CA3B171-4C88-4B84-A33C-340B9F28DFF8",
    47	          "host": "work-bastion",
    48	          "account": "",
    49	          "localPort": 1080
    50	        }
    51	      ]
    52	    }
    53	
    54	profiles preserves its array order. Every profile field is required:
    55	
    56	| Field | Requirement |
    57	| --- | --- |
    58	| id | A unique, stable UUID string. It is the runtime and UI identity; host or port changes do not create a second identity. |
    59	| host | The trimmed SSH destination token. It is non-empty, contains no whitespace, and does not begin with -. Its spelling and case are otherwise preserved. |
    60	| account | The trimmed optional SSH account. An empty string is valid. A non-empty value contains neither whitespace nor @. |
    61	| localPort | An integer in 1...65535, unique across the entire persisted profile list. |
    62	
    63	The list is validated as a whole before a write. A failed add or edit MUST leave
    64	the previous store intact; a duplicate port is rejected even when neither profile
    65	is running. The product MUST NOT choose an ephemeral port, silently substitute
    66	another port, or derive a port from the host.
    67	
    68	Runtime state is deliberately absent from this payload. The app MUST NOT persist
    69	connection state, process IDs, port reservations, diagnostics, or a reconnect
    70	intent.
    71	
    72	If the payload has an unknown newer schemaVersion, invalid required fields, or
    73	invalid JSON, the app MUST preserve it without overwriting it, report a profile
    74	persistence error, and refuse to start affected profiles. It MUST NOT fall back
    75	to the legacy keys or silently create replacement profiles. A separately
    76	confirmed reset MAY replace the invalid payload with an empty v1 store.
    77	
    78	### 2.2 One-time migration from the single-tunnel preferences
    79	
    80	The completion marker is the Boolean legacySocksProfiles.migration.v1.complete.
    81	Migration is idempotent and follows this order on startup:
    82	
    83	1. If a valid v1 payload already exists, load it. If the marker is absent, set
    84	   the marker; do not create another profile.
    85	2. If no v1 payload exists and the marker is absent, read legacy sshHost,
    86	   sshAccount, and localPort. A missing value uses the current single-tunnel
    87	   default for that field: relux, administrator, or 1080.
    88	3. Trim and validate the resulting tuple with the v1 rules. When valid, write a
    89	   v1 payload containing exactly one newly generated profile. When invalid or
    90	   stored with an incompatible type, write an empty v1 payload and surface a
    91	   bounded migration diagnostic rather than creating a partial profile.
    92	4. Persist the v1 payload before setting the completion marker. A restart after
    93	   a crash between those writes follows step 1, so it cannot duplicate a
    94	   profile.
    95	5. Once the marker is true, legacy keys are never read for migration again.
    96	   This prevents deleted profiles from being resurrected on a later launch.
    97	
    98	If the marker is already true but the v1 payload is absent, initialize an empty
    99	v1 payload; do not revisit legacy keys.
   100	
   101	Migration MUST leave sshHost, sshAccount, and localPort untouched for rollback
   102	compatibility with the old application. The new implementation never writes
   103	those legacy keys. An intentionally empty v1 profile list is valid and is not
   104	repopulated from legacy preferences.
   105	
   106	## 3. SSH destination and OpenSSH semantics
   107	
   108	### 3.1 Destination input
   109	
   110	host is one opaque destination argument for /usr/bin/ssh; it is not resolved or
   111	classified by Relux Proxy. The same field therefore accepts all of the following
   112	when OpenSSH accepts them:
   113	
   114	| User input | Required handling |
   115	| --- | --- |
   116	| 203.0.113.10 or an IPv6 literal | Pass it as the destination token; do not turn it into an alias or perform app-side DNS resolution. |
   117	| bastion.example.net | Pass it as the destination token; system OpenSSH resolves it and applies matching configuration. |
   118	| work-bastion where Host work-bastion exists in ~/.ssh/config | Pass the alias unchanged. The system client evaluates that Host stanza and applicable Include or wildcard rules. |
   119	
   120	The runner MUST use process argument APIs, never a shell command string. It
   121	MUST NOT split a destination, synthesize an SSH configuration file, or inspect
   122	~/.ssh/config to choose a key, port, jump host, or resolved hostname.
   123	
   124	### 3.2 Account precedence
   125	
   126	The normalized account field has exactly two modes:
   127	
   128	| Account value | Required invocation behavior | Resulting User precedence |
   129	| --- | --- | --- |
   130	| Empty | Omit -l and pass the bare host destination. Do not construct @host. | User from a matching OpenSSH configuration stanza applies; if none applies, OpenSSH uses its normal system/default login-user behavior. |
   131	| Non-empty | Pass -l account as a separate command-line option and pass the bare host destination. | The command-line account explicitly overrides configured User; no app-side configuration is changed. |
   132	
   133	For example, with Host work-bastion, User deploy, HostName 10.0.0.8, and Port
   134	2202 in ~/.ssh/config, an empty account connects as OpenSSH's configured deploy
   135	user. An account of ops connects as ops; the alias still supplies its hostname
   136	and port.
   137	
   138	### 3.3 What the system client owns
   139	
   140	Each launch retains the current SOCKS command behavior:
   141	
   142	    /usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<localPort>
   143	      -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes
   144	      -o ServerAliveInterval=30
   145	      -o ServerAliveCountMax=3
   146	      -o ConnectTimeout=15
   147	      -o BatchMode=yes
   148	      -o LogLevel=ERROR
   149	      [-l <account>]
   150	      <host>
   151	
   152	Apart from those fixed operational options and the optional account override,
   153	the installed OpenSSH client retains normal precedence and configuration
   154	behavior. In particular, a matching alias MAY provide HostName, Port,
   155	IdentityFile, ProxyJump, and related standard options. Relux Proxy MUST NOT add
   156	command-line overrides for those settings.
   157	
   158	Relux Proxy MUST NOT pass StrictHostKeyChecking, UserKnownHostsFile,
   159	GlobalKnownHostsFile, or another host-key-policy override. Existing known_hosts
   160	files, system defaults, and the user's OpenSSH configuration remain
   161	authoritative. BatchMode=yes preserves the current non-interactive product
   162	behavior; the app does not provide a host-key prompt, approve a key, or weaken
   163	host-key verification.
   164	
   165	## 4. Local port ownership
   166	
   167	Every dynamic forward MUST bind exactly 127.0.0.1:<localPort>. IPv6 loopback,
   168	localhost, all interfaces, LAN interfaces, 0.0.0.0, and every non-loopback
   169	address are prohibited. The app MUST NOT enable a public proxy through
   170	GatewayPorts or an equivalent option.
   171	
   172	The profile registry owns the requested port while that profile is starting,
   173	connected, or stopping. A start MUST reserve the profile and port in the
   174	in-process registry before spawning SSH, so duplicate start requests cannot
   175	create two children for the same profile. The implementation MAY probe whether
   176	127.0.0.1:<localPort> is available before launch, but that probe is advisory:
   177	the SSH process with ExitOnForwardFailure=yes is authoritative because a race
   178	with another local process remains possible.
   179	
   180	If a port is occupied by another profile, another application, or a surviving
   181	child from an unclean prior exit, only the requested profile enters failed. The
   182	app MUST NOT stop, reconfigure, replace, or kill a process it did not spawn in
   183	the current run. Its diagnostic identifies the local endpoint and asks the user
   184	to stop the owner or choose a different port. No other tunnel is affected.
   185	
   186	## 5. Concurrent lifecycle contract
   187	
   188	### 5.1 Independent runtime instances
   189	
   190	The runtime registry is keyed by profile UUID. Each entry owns one immutable
   191	configuration snapshot, one SSH child, one diagnostic buffer, and one lifecycle
   192	generation token. Completion, readiness, stderr, and termination callbacks MUST
   193	check that token before changing state; a late callback from an old child cannot
   194	mark a restarted or removed profile as connected or failed.
   195	
   196	Each profile has these visible states:
   197	
   198	    disconnected -> connecting -> connected -> disconnecting -> disconnected
   199	                        |             |
   200	                        +-----------> failed -> connecting
   201	
   202	An explicit stop during connecting is cancellation and follows the
   203	disconnecting -> disconnected path. An unexpected child exit, forward failure,
   204	readiness timeout, invalid configuration, or unavailable port moves only that
   205	profile to failed. A profile remains failed until the user explicitly starts it
   206	again; there is no automatic retry or reconnect.
   207	
   208	connected requires both that the current-generation SSH child is still running
   209	and that the expected 127.0.0.1:<localPort> endpoint accepts a connection. If
   210	the child exits, its termination result wins over a racing readiness success.
   211	The readiness timeout is 20 seconds, preserving the current 100 probes at
   212	200 ms contract.
   213	
   214	Starting, stopping, or failing one profile MUST NOT change another profile's
   215	state, child process, diagnostic buffer, or configuration. Two or more profiles
   216	with unique ports may therefore be connected at the same time.
   217	
   218	### 5.2 Editing and removal
   219	
   220	An active profile (connecting, connected, or disconnecting) is immutable. The
   221	UI and model MUST reject host, account, and port edits while it is active; they
   222	MUST NOT silently retarget, restart, or mutate a running child.
   223	
   224	Removal is also rejected while a profile is active. The user must explicitly
   225	stop it and wait until it is disconnected or a fully cleaned-up failed state
   226	before removal. This prevents an orphaned SSH child from losing its owning
   227	profile and accidental port reuse while an old process is exiting.
   228	
   229	For an inactive profile, an edit validates the complete collection (including
   230	port uniqueness) and persists atomically. A subsequent start uses the saved
   231	snapshot. Removing an inactive profile removes only its persistent record and
   232	runtime entry; it does not alter any other profile.
   233	
   234	### 5.3 Stop, quit, and restart
   235	
   236	An explicit stop sends a termination request to that profile's current child
   237	and enters disconnecting. After two seconds, the app MAY escalate to a forced
   238	termination only for that same child handle if it is still running. It MUST
   239	observe termination before releasing the runtime reservation and report
   240	disconnected for a user-requested stop.
   241	
   242	On normal app quit, the app applies the same stop procedure to every child it
   243	owns. It MUST never discover and kill processes merely by port, host, or command
   244	line. A child that survived an unclean app crash is not considered owned after a
   245	restart; a later port conflict is handled as the isolated failure in section 4.
   246	
   247	After every launch, all persisted profiles begin disconnected, with no
   248	persisted diagnostic or connection intent. The app MUST NOT automatically
   249	reconnect, restore active tunnels, or modify another process to recreate the
   250	pre-quit state.
   251	
   252	## 6. Diagnostics and failure isolation
   253	
   254	Diagnostics are per profile and runtime-only. Starting a profile clears its
   255	prior diagnostic. Stderr from that profile's SSH child and generated launch or
   256	readiness errors append only to its buffer. After each append, the product MUST
   257	retain only the trailing 4,000 user-visible characters, matching the current
   258	single-tunnel bound.
   259	
   260	On an unexpected exit, the failure summary is the last non-empty diagnostic line
   261	when available, otherwise the SSH exit status. A user-requested stop clears the
   262	diagnostic after termination. Diagnostics MUST NOT be shared across profiles,
   263	written as a persistent profile field, uploaded, or used as telemetry.
   264	
   265	## 7. Explicit non-goals
   266	
   267	This contract does **not** authorize:
   268	
   269	- automatic reconnect, retry backoff, or restoration of tunnels after launch;
   270	- mutation of macOS system proxy, PAC, browser, DNS, or routing settings;
   271	- VPN behavior, NETunnelProviderManager, a Network Extension, packet-tunnel
   272	  entitlements, or system-wide traffic capture/routing;
   273	- editing, generating, importing, or rewriting ~/.ssh/config, known_hosts, SSH
   274	  keys, passphrases, or identities;
   275	- an embedded SSH client or replacement for /usr/bin/ssh;
   276	- public, LAN, IPv6 local-listener, or non-loopback SOCKS exposure;
   277	- automatic local-port allocation or a shared port for multiple profiles.
   278	
   279	Future VPN work has separate product, architecture, security, entitlement, and
   280	release gates. It does not implicitly inherit this legacy SOCKS persistence or
   281	OpenSSH-alias contract.
   282	
   283	## 8. Required implementation evidence
   284	
   285	An implementation of this contract MUST demonstrate, with automated tests or
   286	deterministic fakes where a live SSH server is unnecessary:
   287	
   288	1. migration of valid legacy values into one v1 profile, idempotence across a
   289	   restart, invalid-input handling, and non-resurrection after deleting all
   290	   profiles;
   291	2. exact account behavior for an IP, DNS hostname, and Host alias: empty account
   292	   omits -l, while a non-empty account passes -l and overrides only User;
   293	3. rejection of duplicate, invalid, and externally occupied local ports without
   294	   interrupting an already connected profile;
   295	4. independent concurrent start, failure, stop, and diagnostic buffers for at
   296	   least two unique ports, including stale-callback protection;
   297	5. rejection of active-profile edits and removal, orderly normal-quit child
   298	   termination, and a restart with every profile disconnected and no auto-retry.
   299	
   300	## 9. Version history
   301	
   302	| Version | Date | Change |
   303	| --- | --- | --- |
   304	| 1.1 | 2026-07-28 | Recorded the implemented v0.2.0 multi-profile behavior as shipped product scope while keeping GitLab publication explicitly gated. |
   305	| 1.0 | 2026-07-28 | Initial contract for the planned multi-profile legacy SOCKS extension. |

